# config/Kconfig.limits — Resource cap table (subsumes RFC-0023)
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Every static-array bound from across the codebase
# is declared here with profile-derived defaults.
# Phase C3 will replace the scattered `pub const` sites with generated.rs.

menu "Resource Limits"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_TASKS
    int "Maximum schedulable tasks"
    range 8 16384
    default 32   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64   if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Number of task slots: processes and threads share this one static
      table.  Everything below is allocated at build time for all MAX_TASKS
      slots whether or not a task exists, and none of it is heap.  Scheduler
      walks (per-tick WCET) and the page-table tracker (PT_META) scan linearly
      in MAX_TASKS.

      Cost: ~38,573 B of RAM per task slot (riscv64), 39,757 B (aarch64), at
      the edge values of KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB = 32 and MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK = 256.
      Of that: 32,768 B kernel stack (KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB, TASK_STACKS);
      2,052 B capability table (8 B x MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK + 4, CAP_TABLES);
      2,048 B run queues (MAX_CPUS 8 x NUM_PRIORITIES 32 x one 8-byte slot;
      wave 15: now a 4 B ready-queue link, RQ_NEXT, the queues being lists
      -- 2,044 B less per slot than the figures here, which predate it);
      the task control block (TASKS), 1,216 B riscv64 / 1,856 B aarch64, and
      on aarch64 544 B of lazy FP save area; 268 B signal state; 80 B topology
      slot; 24 B each of notify waiter and vDSO slot; 16 B each of port waiter
      and page-table tracker slot; 12 B each of lease waiter and exit notice
      (EXIT_NOTE: one per slot since wave 12, which retired the 32-entry
      SCHED_EXIT_NOTES); 8 B TID map, nine 4-byte
      side tables and five 1-byte flags.  All of it is .bss except the
      topology, port, lease and TID tables, which are in .data.
      At the default, riscv64 / aarch64: edge (64 tasks) 2.35 / 2.43 MiB;
      embedded (32 tasks, 32 caps per task) 1.12 / 1.16 MiB; fleet (4096 tasks,
      512 caps per task) 158.7 / 163.3 MiB.  Measured with
      tools/limits_cost.py; the costs of the options that size a table per task
      are in their own help.  The TID map is next_power_of_two(MAX_TASKS) x 8 B,
      so 64 -> 65 adds 512 B at once.

      Not counted here, taken from the page allocator at run time: one 4 KiB
      vDSO frame per slot a task has used (kept until reboot; the memory
      admission reserve counts it), and each ring-3 task's own frames
      (USER_STACK_SIZE_KB, image, page tables), charged to its mem_pages.

      Ceilings in the code: MAX_TASKS < 65,535 (TID_SLOT and the port waiter
      lists store slot numbers as u16; compile-time asserts in scheduler.rs and
      port.rs; resident_histogram.rs asserts < 65,536); the Kconfig range stops
      at 16,384.  The signed topology keeps its row count in a u8
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, tasks_len): the 256th declared
      task row overflows that counter, a panic because the release profile
      has overflow checks on, however large MAX_TASKS is.
      The build refuses MAX_FDS_TOTAL < 4 x MAX_TASKS, MAX_CAPS_TOTAL <
      8 x MAX_TASKS and MAX_SUBS_PER_TOPIC > MAX_TASKS.

config NR_CPUS
    int "Maximum number of CPUs (ceiling, like Linux CONFIG_NR_CPUS)"
    range 1 64
    default 8  if BOARD_K1
    default 5  if BOARD_VF2
    default 4  if BOARD_RPI5
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      The largest CPU id plus one this kernel can run.  A CPU id is the hart
      id on riscv64 (tp, the value SBI hands each hart; the VF2 enumerates
      its S7 monitor core as hart 0 and the four U74s as 1..4, so it needs 5)
      and the boot-assigned logical id on aarch64 (TPIDR_EL1, 0..n-1).

      At boot the kernel counts the DTB's /cpus nodes: that count, cut to
      this ceiling, is nr_cpu_ids and the possible-CPU mask.  A DTB that
      names more CPUs than this boots on the first NR_CPUS with a kwarn line
      ("[SMP] DTB names N CPUs, NR_CPUS is M"); the rest are never started.

      Per-CPU state lives in per-CPU areas the kernel allocates from the
      frame allocator at boot, one per possible CPU, before the secondaries
      start (crates/core/percpu).  Raising this ceiling does not add areas;
      it widens the tables that must stay static (the per-CPU pointer
      tables, the CPU masks, and the small tables assembly or early boot
      indexes by CPU id: see ARCHITECTURE.md, "SMP and per-CPU state").

      Cost, per unit of this option, in the image (measured, qemu
      configuration, NR_CPUS 8 against 64): 466 B on riscv64, 349 B on
      aarch64 -- the per-CPU pointer tables, masks, preemption and IRQ-depth
      counters, slab magazine pointers and the stack tables the asm reads.
      Per possible CPU, at run time, from the frame allocator: one per-CPU
      area -- the 32 ready-queue heads (512 B; the queues are lists whose
      links live in one MAX_TASKS-long table, 4 B a task), the RT band's
      1,080 B, the trace producer's 64 B, and 6,584 B of APS tables when
      that backend is built -- one 4 KiB page (8 KiB with APS) in every
      profile, MAX_TASKS does not change it; plus, for each CPU but the
      boot CPU, SECONDARY_STACK_SIZE_KB + INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB of stacks.

      The range stops at 64: task.cpu_affinity is an i8, the online-prefix
      bookkeeping (crates/core/sched/src/hart_set.rs) is one u64, and the
      energy model's domain masks (crates/core/energy) cover CPUs 0..31
      only; each is a compile-time assert.  Set it to the board's CPU
      count to spend nothing on CPUs that cannot exist.

config SECONDARY_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Boot stack of each secondary CPU (KiB)"
    range 8 256
    default 16
    help
      The stack a secondary CPU runs its bring-up and idle loop on, until
      its first task switch.  Allocated at boot in that CPU's per-CPU area,
      only for possible CPUs other than the boot CPU (which runs on the
      linker-reserved boot stack, BOOT_STACK_SIZE).  16 KiB is the size the
      kernel has always used: enough for nested 288 B trap frames, the
      scheduler and its Rust callers.

      Cost: this value per possible CPU but one, at run time; nothing in
      the image.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config TCP_MAX_CONNS
    int "Maximum simultaneous TCP connections"
    range 1 65536
    default 4    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64   if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 8    if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 1024 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Static connection-control-block (CCB) array size.  DOMAIN_GATEWAY
      on the Edge profile starts it at 64, with TCP_BUF_SIZE 16384.  Each entry
      costs TCP_BUF_SIZE × 2 bytes of buffer memory (RX + TX) plus
      6,088 B of connection state, 4 × 1,468 B of it the out-of-order
      queue: size_of::<TcpConn>() (crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs) is 268,232 B
      at TCP_BUF_SIZE = 131072 and 38,856 B at 16384.  TCP_BUF_SIZE is
      per profile (config/Kconfig.network), so the static table is
      4 × 268,232 B embedded, 8 × 268,232 B edge and 1024 × 38,856 B
      (37.9 MiB) fleet.
      Must be at most MAX_SOCKETS - 4 (the build refuses less).

      Cost: 2 x TCP_BUF_SIZE + 6,088 B of RAM per connection, the same on
      riscv64 and aarch64: 268,232 B at the 131072-byte ring, 38,856 B at
      16384.  At the default: edge (8 connections) 2,145,888 B = 2.05 MiB;
      embedded (4) 1,072,960 B = 1.02 MiB; fleet (1024, 16384-byte rings)
      39,788,576 B = 37.9 MiB.  The table is in .data (its initial state is not
      all zero), so it is in the image file as well as in RAM.  A connection
      lookup scans the table (with a one-entry hint).  No code ceiling below
      the Kconfig range.

config MAX_SOCKETS
    int "Maximum total sockets (TCP + UDP)"
    range 1 65536
    default 8    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128  if DOMAIN_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16   if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 2048 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Combined pool for all socket types: the table of socket descriptors.
      TCP connection state is not in it (see TCP_MAX_CONNS and TCP_BUF_SIZE),
      and the UDP datagram rings are not in it either: UDP has its own fixed
      table of 8 sockets (UDP_MAX_SOCKETS, a literal in
      crates/net/net/src/udp.rs, not a Kconfig symbol), each with
      UDP_RX_SLOTS datagram slots.
      DOMAIN_GATEWAY on the Edge profile starts it at 128.
      Must be at least TCP_MAX_CONNS + 4 (the build refuses less).  One task
      may hold half of them (MAX_SOCKETS_PER_TASK).

      Cost: 44 B of RAM per socket slot (SOCKS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64.  At the default: edge (16) 708 B; embedded (8) 352 B; fleet
      (2048) 90,116 B = 88 KiB.  No code ceiling below the Kconfig range.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# File descriptors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_FDS_PER_PROC
    int "File descriptors per process"
    range 4 4096
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Size of the kernel's file-descriptor table, for the WHOLE MACHINE.
      `crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs` aliases this to `MAX_FDS`, the length of the
      one shared array — the name says "per proc" and the option has never
      controlled a per-process anything.

      The real per-task ceiling is `MAX_FDS_PER_TASK` in that same file,
      derived as half of this, mirroring `MAX_SOCKETS_PER_TASK`. Until
      2026-09-06 there was no per-task ceiling at all and one ring-3
      program could take every slot.

      Not renamed here: the name reaches `.config` files and the generated
      constants, so changing it is a separate, mechanical change. The help
      text is corrected so the next reader is not misled by it.

      Includes FAT32 files and device nodes. NOT sockets — those live in a
      separate table with a separate size (`MAX_SOCKETS`), which is the
      other half of why this text misled.
      Current codebase value: 16 (edge, crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs MAX_FDS).

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per slot (KERNEL_FD_TABLE, .data), the same on riscv64
      and aarch64.  At the default: edge (16) 408 B; embedded (8) 216 B; fleet
      (256) 6,168 B.  The file-capability resource numbers start at 0x4000_0000
      (crates/core/ipc/src/file_cap.rs), asserted above this value; the range
      stops at 4096.

config MAX_FDS_TOTAL
    int "Global file descriptor table size"
    range 16 262144
    default 128   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16384 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      System-wide FD table.  The build-time validator in
      `crates/core/limits/build.rs` enforces:
          MAX_FDS_TOTAL >= MAX_TASKS * 4
      (average-case budget: ~4 FDs per task — stdin/stdout/stderr + one
      socket).  MAX_FDS_PER_PROC is the per-task ceiling and does NOT
      enter this formula directly.

      Defaults are a budget per profile, not a strict `MAX_TASKS * 4`
      computation (that formula is only the build.rs FLOOR, checked as
      `>=`, not the value chosen here):
        embedded (MAX_TASKS=32)   -> 128    (4x  the floor)
        edge     (MAX_TASKS=64)   -> 2048   (32x the floor)
        fleet    (MAX_TASKS=4096) -> 16384  (4x  the floor)
      A higher value changes nothing but this check. (Fixed
      2026-09-26, V2.6: this previously said "edge (512 tasks)" against
      MAX_TASKS' own PROFILE_EDGE default of 64 — stale copy-paste, not a
      real 512-task edge configuration that ever existed.)

      Cost: none.  No table is sized by this option: it is read only by the
      build.rs checks above (measured: +0 B of RAM for 2048 -> 2049 and
      -> 4096, on both ISAs).  The file-descriptor table that exists is the
      one MAX_FDS_PER_PROC sizes, at 24 B per slot.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IPC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_CHANNELS
    int "Maximum IPC channels"
    range 4 65536
    default 16   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16   if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Static channel table size.  Each channel is a fixed-capacity
      message queue.  Current codebase value: 16 (crates/core/ipc/src/channel.rs).
      Note: behavior/brain_protocol.rs uses MAX_CHANNELS = 8 for a
      different purpose — subsumption layer count, not IPC channel count.

      Cost: 572 B of RAM per channel (POOL, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64.  At the default: edge and embedded (16) 9,156 B = 8.9 KiB;
      fleet (4096) 2,342,916 B = 2.23 MiB.  One task may own half of them
      (MAX_CHANNELS_PER_TASK).  The Cap<Channel> handle packs the slot as
      ceil(log2(MAX_CHANNELS)) index bits and the rest generation (crates/core/
      ipc/src/objref.rs, asserted at build): 65,536 channels, the range limit,
      leave 16 generation bits.

config MAX_PIPES
    int "Maximum IPC pipes"
    range 4 65536
    default 8    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32   if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 1024 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Static pipe (byte-stream IPC) table size.  Each pipe holds a
      fixed-size ring buffer.  Current codebase value: 32.

      Cost: 4,148 B of RAM per pipe (PIPES: the 4,096-byte ring, PIPE_BUF_SIZE,
      plus 52 B of state), the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default:
      edge (32) 132,744 B = 129.6 KiB; embedded (8) 33,192 B = 32.4 KiB; fleet
      (1024) 4,247,560 B = 4.05 MiB.  The table is in .data, so it is in the
      image file as well as in RAM.  No code ceiling below the Kconfig range.

config MAX_PORTS
    int "Maximum IPC ports (rendezvous endpoints)"
    range 4 65536
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Named port table size.  Ports are used for capability-based
      rendezvous.  Current codebase value: 32 (crates/core/ipc/src/port.rs).

      Cost: 816 B of RAM per port (PORTS, .data), of which 768 B are the two
      arrays sized by MAX_PORT_SOURCES (48 B x 16 at its default), plus a
      16-byte waiter slot per task slot that is counted under MAX_TASKS; the
      same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge (32) 27,144 B = 26.5
      KiB including the waiter slots of 64 tasks; embedded (8) 7,048 B
      including the waiter slots of 32 tasks; fleet (512) 483,336 B = 472 KiB
      including the waiter slots of 4096 tasks.  The Cap<Port> handle packs the slot as
      ceil(log2(MAX_PORTS)) index bits and the rest generation (objref.rs,
      asserted at build); 65,536 ports leave 16 generation bits.

config MAX_PORT_SOURCES
    int "Maximum event sources bound to one port"
    range 2 64
    default 16
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/port.rs, PORT_MAX_SOURCES: the channel, io_ring
      and timer sources one port can hold (SYS_PORT_BIND_TYPED answers
      -EMFILE past it), and the depth of its queue of IRQ events. Each
      port carries both arrays, 48 bytes per entry: with MAX_PORTS = 512
      (the fleet default) the value 64 costs 1.5 MiB of static memory and
      the default 16 costs 384 KiB; with 32 ports (edge), 96 KiB at 64.
      A remove by key lists the removed sources on the syscall stack,
      28 bytes each (1.8 KiB at 64).

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per entry per port (PORTS, .data: both arrays
      together), i.e. 48 x MAX_PORTS B per unit of this option (1,536 B at
      edge's 32 ports), the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default 16:
      edge (32 ports) 24 KiB; embedded (8) 6 KiB; fleet (512) 384 KiB, all of
      it inside the PORTS figure of MAX_PORTS.  It is a stack and table size
      only: no frame is taken from the page allocator.

config MAX_LEASES
    int "Maximum IPC memory leases"
    range 4 65536
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Lease table for shared-memory delegation between tasks.  Each
      lease maps a physical region into another task's address space
      for a bounded time.  Current codebase value: 16.

      Cost: 112 B of RAM per lease (LEASES, .data: the grant, its mapping
      record and its seal record), plus a 12-byte accept-waiter slot per task
      slot that is counted under MAX_TASKS; the same on riscv64 and aarch64.
      At the default: edge (16) 2,568 B including the waiter slots of 64
      tasks; embedded (8) 1,288 B including the waiter slots of 32 tasks;
      fleet (512) 106,504 B = 104 KiB including the waiter slots of 4096
      tasks.  One task may lend half of them (MAX_LEASES_PER_LESSOR).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pub/Sub
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_SERVICES
    int "Maximum named services"
    range 4 65536
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Service-registry table size.  Services are typed endpoints
      discoverable by name.  Current codebase value: 32 (crates/core/service).

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per service (SERVICE_TABLE, .bss), the same on riscv64
      and aarch64.  At the default: edge (32) 1,552 B; embedded (8) 384 B;
      fleet (512) 24,592 B = 24 KiB.

config MAX_TOPICS
    int "Maximum pub/sub topics"
    range 4 65536
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Static topic table size.  Each topic holds a fixed-size message
      ring plus subscriber list.  Current codebase value: 32 (crates/core/pubsub).

      Cost: 312 B of RAM per topic plus 8 B per subscriber slot
      (MAX_SUBS_PER_TOPIC), so 376 B per topic at 8 subscribers (TOPICS, .data);
      the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge (32 x 8 slots)
      12,040 B = 11.8 KiB; embedded (8 x 4) 2,752 B; fleet (512 x 32)
      290,824 B = 284 KiB.  The build also requires KERNEL_HEAP_SIZE to cover
      MAX_TOPICS x 512 B plus the other terms of its heap floor (see
      KERNEL_HEAP_SIZE).

config MAX_SUBS_PER_TOPIC
    int "Maximum subscribers per topic"
    range 1 256
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 32 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Fan-out limit per topic.  Each subscription slot holds a task ID
      and a read pointer.  Current codebase value: 8 (crates/core/pubsub).

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per subscriber slot per topic, i.e. 8 x MAX_TOPICS B per
      unit of this option (256 B at edge's 32 topics), inside TOPICS (.data);
      the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  The build refuses a value above
      MAX_TASKS.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Capability system
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_CAPS_TOTAL
    int "Total capability pool size"
    range 64 1048576
    default 256   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 1024  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 65536 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Size of the capability-declaration pool of the signed topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, caps_pool): the number of `caps`
      entries all task rows of CAPS.TOML together may declare.  It is not the
      run-time capability table: each task's table has MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK slots
      (see there).  The build requires it to be at least MAX_TASKS x 8 and at
      least MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry (the topology state, SLOT, .data), the same
      on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge (1024) 24,576 B = 24 KiB;
      embedded (256) 6,144 B; fleet (65,536) 1.5 MiB.  Ceiling in the code: the
      pool length is a u16 (caps_pool_len), so at most 65,535 entries can be
      used; the Kconfig range (1,048,576) is above it, and the fleet default
      of 65,536 is one over.

config SYSCALL_FILTER_MAX
    int "Syscall filter entries per image profile"
    range 99 255
    default 128
    help
      Size of each task's allow-list of system calls (per-image seccomp
      profile, crates/core/sched/src/filter.rs). The widest profile must
      leave a quarter of the list free (checked by host seccomp-tests), so
      the next profile that grows trips in a test, not on a board.

      Cost: 2 bytes per entry in every task slot. The filter check itself
      costs no instructions at any size: the dispatcher answers from the
      640-bit bitmap, and only a number past the bitmap (never a real
      syscall) scans the list, stopping at the profile's own count. The
      Task slot does grow: 128 entries take riscv64's Task from 1216 to
      1280 bytes (aarch64 1856 to 1920), and on riscv64 LLVM lowers the
      slot multiply by 1280 to two shifts and an add instead of li+mul,
      +1 instruction on getpid (vsbench syscall-floor 180 -> 181).

      Floor 99: the widest profile (ABITEST.ELF) lists 74, and 74 is three
      quarters of 99. Ceiling 255: the count is a u8. One default for every
      ISA and profile: the same images, so the same widest profile, ship
      on all of them.

config MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK
    int "Maximum capabilities per task"
    range 8 512
    default 32  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 256 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Per-task capability slot count.  Limits how many IPC endpoints,
      memory regions, and devices a single process can hold at once.
      Current codebase value: 256 (crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs).

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per slot per task slot, i.e. 8 x MAX_TASKS B per unit of
      this option (512 B at edge's 64 tasks), in CAP_TABLES (.bss, 4 B of lock
      per task on top); the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge
      (256 slots x 64 tasks) 131,328 B = 128.3 KiB; embedded (32 x 32) 8,320 B;
      fleet (512 x 4096) 16,793,600 B = 16.0 MiB.
      Ceiling: 512.  A CapHandle addresses a slot with 9 bits
      (CapHandle::SLOT_BITS, crates/core/abi/src/cap.rs) and a compile-time
      assert in crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs fails the build above 512 (tried:
      513), so the range stops there.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory budgets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config KERNEL_HEAP_SIZE
    int "Kernel heap size (KiB)"
    range 64 1048576
    default 512    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32768  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 262144 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Heap region the kernel reserves at boot right after _kernel_end
      (kernel/src/entry/{riscv64,aarch64}/boot_hooks.rs), not part of the image.  It must be free RAM:
      the image, this heap, and the PMM's page-table and task frames
      share the board's RAM above the firmware.
      Edge default: 32 MiB.  Embedded: 512 KiB.  Fleet: 256 MiB.
      Embedded figures (image, what is left on 16 and 64 MiB boards)
      are printed by tools/size_report.sh.

      Cost: no static RAM (the image does not grow: +0 B measured for 32768 ->
      32769 and -> 65536 KiB, both ISAs).  The heap is taken from the page
      allocator at boot, so every KiB here is a KiB less for ring-3 frames.
      What lives in it today: tmpfs file data (up to TMPFS_MAX_KB), procfs
      text and the boot-time self-tests.  None of the tables the options in
      this file size is in the heap: they are static (.data/.bss), so the
      MAX_TASKS x 384 + ... floor that build.rs checks is a conservative
      estimate of a heap that those tables no longer use, not a measurement.

config KHEAP_SLAB
    bool "Per-CPU size-class cache in front of the kernel heap"
    default y
    help
      Wave 15 (SLAB). Small kernel heap requests (up to KHEAP_SLAB_MAX_CLASS
      bytes) are served from per-CPU magazines of fixed size classes, a
      per-class depot of full and empty magazines, and slabs carved from the
      heap (crates/core/mm/src/slab.rs). Alloc and free of a small object
      are then O(1) with no lock and no heap walk while the CPU's magazines
      hold or have room for it; a miss costs at most one magazine of slab
      work with interrupts masked, never a heap walk. Larger requests, and
      every request with this off, go to the first-fit heap exactly as
      before (off is today's allocator, kept for comparison).

      Measured (riscv64, -icount, wave 15): the kernel heap is not on any
      vsbench hot path (spawn, thread, fork, IPC, file, UDP make no heap
      allocation); procfs text on open is its main user. So this moves no
      lane; it bounds what an allocation costs. A heap refusal first gives
      back what this layer caches (this CPU's magazines, the depot, empty
      slabs) and retries once; a refusal is still a refusal.

      Cost: 3,712 B of .data (riscv64, measured: 8 CPUs x 2 x 16 magazine
      pointers and 17 class locks) and about 0.5 KiB of tables, plus, in the
      heap, what the options below let it cache (edge defaults: at most
      27 KiB per CPU, 54 KiB of depot, one 4-32 KiB slab per class in use).
      Instructions (riscv64, -icount): a small alloc or free that its
      magazine serves is about 40 (an alloc+free pair 81) against 310-360
      for the first-fit heap on a nearly empty heap and 1,700+ with 256
      small holes in front of the request; a request above the largest class
      pays about 7 more than before (the interrupt-context owner check in
      crates/core/mm/src/kheap.rs).
      Same on both ISAs: nothing here depends on the page granule.
      The KHEAP_SLAB_* options below are read only with this on.

config KHEAP_SLAB_CLASSES
    string "Size classes (bytes, ascending, multiples of 8, at most 16)"
    default "16 32 64 128 256 512 1024 2048"
    help
      The object sizes the cache serves; a request gets the smallest class
      that holds it. The default is powers of two because that is what the
      measured traffic supports: on the abitest workload 99% of
      allocations were 56 or 64 bytes (procfs text on open), the rest
      scattered between 8 and 264 bytes, with no intermediate size frequent
      enough to pay for a class of its own (a class costs at least one slab
      of heap once used). Add one (48, 96, ...) when a census
      (`kheap-census`) shows a size worth it. Each class is naturally
      aligned to its largest power-of-two divisor; a request aligned beyond
      that moves to a larger class or to the heap.

config KHEAP_SLAB_MAX_CLASS
    int "Largest request served by the cache (bytes)"
    range 16 4096
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048
    help
      Classes in KHEAP_SLAB_CLASSES above this are dropped; larger requests
      go to the heap. Lower it to keep big objects out of slabs (a slab of a
      2048-byte class is 32 KiB holding 15 objects). Embedded keeps it at
      256 so no slab exceeds 2 KiB of its 512 KiB heap.

config KHEAP_SLAB_MAG_DEPTH
    int "Magazine depth (objects)"
    range 1 1024
    default 8  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Most objects one magazine holds. Each CPU has two per class, so this
      many alloc/free in a row (twice it, with the swap) stay on the
      lock-free path; a deeper magazine amortises the depot lock over more
      operations and caches more memory. Per class the depth is further cut
      by KHEAP_SLAB_CPU_CACHE_KB. A magazine is 24 + 8 x depth bytes of heap.

config KHEAP_SLAB_CPU_CACHE_KB
    int "Most memory one CPU caches in magazines (KiB)"
    range 1 65536
    default 2   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Split evenly between classes; a class's depth is the smaller of
      KHEAP_SLAB_MAG_DEPTH and its share / (2 x size), at least 1. This is
      the memory a CPU can hold that no other CPU and no reclaim can take
      back (only that CPU's own refusal path drains it).

config KHEAP_SLAB_DEPOT_MAGS
    int "Full magazines kept per class in the depot"
    range 0 256
    default 1 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4
    help
      The depot moves objects between CPUs a magazine at a time. A CPU
      whose magazines are full puts one here (when there is room) instead
      of returning its objects to their slabs one by one. Cost: up to this
      many x depth x class size bytes per class, returned on a heap refusal.

config KHEAP_SLAB_BYTES
    int "Smallest slab (bytes, a power of two)"
    range 1024 65536
    default 1024 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8192 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4096
    help
      A class's slab is the smallest power of two from this that holds
      KHEAP_SLAB_MIN_OBJS objects after its 64-byte header. Slabs are taken
      from the heap aligned to their size, so a bigger slab is fewer heap
      walks and more internal waste for a class used lightly.

config KHEAP_SLAB_MIN_OBJS
    int "Fewest objects in a slab"
    range 1 256
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      A slab grows (doubling from KHEAP_SLAB_BYTES) until it holds this many
      objects; it bounds the header-and-tail waste to about 1/this.

config KHEAP_SLAB_KEEP_EMPTY
    int "Empty slabs a class keeps"
    range 0 64
    default 0 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 1
    help
      A slab whose last object is freed goes back to the heap unless its
      class already keeps this many empty ones. Keeping one avoids a heap
      walk on a class that oscillates around a slab boundary; it costs one
      slab per class.

config KHEAP_SLAB_DEBUG
    bool "Poison freed objects and check red zones"
    default y if LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG && BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    help
      A freed small object is filled with 0x6b and checked on its next
      allocation (a write after free panics with the object's address); the
      bytes between the requested size and the class size are filled with
      0xbb and checked on free (an overrun panics). On by default only in
      a development build (BUILD_TYPE) at the debug log level, the QEMU
      development configurations, never in a release build by default.
      Cost: a fill and a scan of the object on every alloc and free (about
      size/8 to size instructions each), nothing in RAM.

config RING3_MEM_PAGES_DEFAULT
    int "Frame budget of a ring-3 topology row that declares none (pages)"
    range 16 1048576
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 1024 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      RFC-0049 P1: every ring-3 row of the signed topology (a row named
      after an .ELF image, or `autorun`) has a frame budget.  A row
      without `mem_pages` gets this one as its ceiling.  The budget
      counts every frame the task holds: ELF image, user stack, page
      tables, heap, anonymous and demand pages, the shared memory it
      creates and its io_ring pages (crates/core/topology/src/memory.rs).
      Boot-time memory admission counts a ceiling row twice (one COW
      copy, P3), so this value times two, per row, must fit the RAM.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); a ceiling in 4 KiB frames
      checked against free RAM at boot (1024 pages = 4 MiB per row).

config AUTORUN_MEM_PAGES
    int "Frame budget of the default topology's autorun row (pages)"
    range 16 1048576
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 2048 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The `autorun` row of the built-in topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/builder.rs), shared by every autorun image
      without a row of its own.  Edge: 2048 pages (8 MiB), four times the
      largest measured peak (vsbench, 512 pages of heap).  Embedded: 256
      pages, so the default topology is admitted on a 16 MiB board.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); a ceiling in 4 KiB frames
      checked against free RAM at boot (2048 pages = 8 MiB).

config MEM_KERNEL_RESERVE_KB
    int "RAM memory admission keeps back for the kernel (KiB)"
    range 0 1048576
    default 256   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16384 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Frames the kernel may still take after boot-time memory admission
      and that no task budget covers: page tables of kernel mappings,
      driver queues set up late, the block cache.  The per-slot vDSO
      pages (one per MAX_TASKS slot) are added on top of this by the
      kernel.  Admission refuses the topology unless the ring-3 budgets
      plus this reserve fit the free RAM (crates/core/topology/src/memory.rs).

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); a number of free frames
      admission keeps back.  The kernel adds one 4 KiB vDSO frame per MAX_TASKS
      slot on top (kernel/src/boot/topology.rs): 256 KiB at edge's 64 tasks.

config MM_REGIONS_PER_SPACE
    int "Demand-paged mmap region records per address space"
    range 2 256
    default 8
    help
      Wave 14 (DEMANDPAGE): an anonymous mmap reserves its range as a
      region record (crates/core/mm/src/region.rs) and commits each page
      on first touch.  Each user address space has this many records, in
      a static table of MAX_TASKS slots (crates/core/mm/src/pager.rs).
      Consecutive mmaps with the same permissions fold into one record,
      so a task that only grows its mappings uses one; mprotect and a
      munmap in the middle of a region cut records.  A mapping that finds
      no free record is committed eagerly instead (the pre-wave-14
      behaviour); a munmap or mprotect whose cut needs a record answers
      ENOMEM, as Linux does past its map count.

      Cost: (24 + 32 x this) B of .bss per MAX_TASKS slot, both ISAs:
      280 B at 8, 17.5 KiB at edge's 64 tasks.

config DMA_POOL_FLOOR_KB
    int "Minimum DMA pool reserved at boot (KiB)"
    range 0 1048576
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 2048 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      RFC-0049 P7: the physically contiguous pool reserved at boot for
      device DMA.  The pool is the larger of this floor and the sum of
      the `dma_kb` of every `[pipeline.NAME]` in the signed topology.
      Reserved only: no driver allocates from it yet.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); the pool is taken from
      the page allocator at boot, 1 KiB of RAM per KiB, so 2048 KiB is 2 MiB
      less for ring-3 frames.

config USER_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "User-task stack size (KiB)"
    range 4 1024
    default 64 if AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    default 16
    help
      Stack of every user task, ending at USER_STACK_TOP.  16 KiB on both
      ISAs (aarch64 was 32 with nothing in the code needing it; owner
      decision 2026-09-28).  Populated
      eagerly at exec (crates/core/sched/src/process.rs): every page is
      allocated, zeroed and mapped read-write before the task runs, so
      each task costs USER_STACK_SIZE_KB / 4 physical frames whether it
      touches them or not.  No guard page is carved out of it or placed
      below it: the bottom of the stack is also the ceiling of the task's
      MMIO/shm window (USER_MMIO_LIMIT), so an overflow can run into a
      mapping there instead of faulting.  Nothing the kernel saves goes
      on this stack: integer registers go to the kernel trap frame, and
      aarch64 FP/NEON state to the kernel's lazy-FP save areas.
      Must be a multiple of 4 (one page); the build refuses any other value.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); USER_STACK_SIZE_KB of
      page-allocator frames per ring-3 task, taken at exec and charged to its
      mem_pages: 16 KiB, so 1 MiB for 64 ring-3 tasks, 64 MiB for 4096.  The
      stack ends at USER_STACK_TOP; there is no code ceiling below the range.

config KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Per-task kernel stack (KiB)"
    range 4 256
    default 32 if ARCH_RISCV64
    default 128 if AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    default 48 if AARCH64_PAGE_16K
    default 32 if ARCH_AARCH64
    default 32 if ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Kernel-mode stack depth reserved when a user task syscalls into
      the kernel.  Sized for the deepest syscall handler chain including
      interrupt nesting.  Larger on Aarch64/x86_64 due to bigger trap frames.

      A guard page (one base page: 4 KiB, or the aarch64 granule) is taken
      out of this, so the usable depth is one page less — hence the larger
      defaults under AARCH64_PAGE_16K (48 = 32 usable) and _64K (128 = 64
      usable). The size must be a whole number of pages.  Since 2026-09-16 an interrupt HANDLER runs on the hart's
      own INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB stack, so this size covers the deepest
      syscall chain and the 288-byte trap frame, and no longer has to leave
      room for a handler on top of it.  The frame itself still lands here.

      MEASURED, not chosen (tools/stack_chain.py, 2026-09-16).  The deepest
      chain from trap_handler is 18.6 KiB on the qemu and embedded images and
      20.6 KiB on fleet, whose trap_handler frame alone is 2.2 KiB:

        dispatch_slow -> sys_recvfrom_syscall (4192) -> recv_bounced (4144)
        -> socket_recv -> tcp::recv -> send_segment_opts (1648)
        -> ip::send_flags (3136) -> ip::handle -> handle_icmp (1552)
        -> icmp_frag_needed -> retransmit (1712)

      `ip::send_flags -> ip::handle` is the loopback re-entry, and it is what
      makes the TCP/IP send path a 14-function mutually recursive component:
      its depth is not statically bounded, and stack_chain.py charges it the
      longest path in which no function repeats.  Splitting recvfrom's bounce
      buffer into its own frame the same day took that chain to 14.6 KiB; with
      288 for the trap frame, 32 KiB (28 KiB usable) leaves about 13 KiB.

      EMBEDDED IS NOT SMALLER FOR NOW.  It ran at 16 KiB -- 12 KiB usable --
      against that same 18.6 KiB chain, because it links the same network
      stack; only its table sizes are smaller.  Two 4 KiB syscall bounce
      buffers are 8.3 KiB of the chain, so moving them off the stack is what
      would let this profile shrink again.

      Cost: MAX_TASKS KiB of RAM per KiB of this option: one stack per task
      slot (TASK_STACKS, .bss), the same on riscv64 and aarch64 (measured:
      +65,536 B per KiB at 64 tasks).  At the default 32 KiB: edge (64 tasks)
      2 MiB; embedded (32) 1 MiB; fleet (4096) 128 MiB.  It is the largest
      single line of the per-task cost under MAX_TASKS (32,768 of 38,573 B on riscv64, 39,757 B on aarch64).
      The build refuses 4 KiB or less (nothing would be left after the guard
      page); the range stops at 256.

config INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Per-hart interrupt stack (KiB)"
    range 2 64
    default 8 if ARCH_RISCV64
    default 8 if ARCH_AARCH64
    # x86_64: the IST stacks (#DF, NMI, #MC, X86_IST_STACK_SIZE_KB each)
    # come out of the top of the same area; 20 leaves riscv64's 8 KiB below.
    default 20 if ARCH_X86_64
    help
      A dedicated stack per hart for interrupt handlers, separate from the
      task kernel stack.  On rv64 it is WIRED UP since 2026-09-16: the trap
      entry switches to it when scause says interrupt, and only when the
      stack in hand is not already this hart's -- a trap taken while on it
      keeps it, so a nested handler does not restart at the top and overwrite
      the outer one.  The 288-byte trap frame stays on the interrupted stack,
      because trap_return computes the next trap's sscratch from it.

      MEASURED (tools/stack_chain.py, 2026-09-16).  The whole interrupt half
      needs 2,400 bytes: 240 for trap_handler's own frame plus 2,160 for the
      deepest chain from the interrupt callees -- irq_dispatch through the
      port queue into wake_task_by_tid, including its panic tail.  Add a
      nested trap frame and 8 KiB leaves about 5 KiB for the drivers a real
      board brings.  There is no guard page here: the slots are .bss, so the
      bottom eight bytes of each carry a magic word that trap_resched checks
      on the way out of every trap.

      The cost is this value once per possible CPU: the boot CPU's stack is
      in .bss, every other CPU's is in its per-CPU area.  What it buys is
      that the deepest syscall chain no longer has to leave room for an
      interrupt on top of it.

      Cost: 1 KiB of RAM per KiB of this option per possible CPU, the same
      on riscv64 and aarch64: 1 KiB of .bss (the boot CPU's stack,
      `boot_irq_stack`), the rest from the frame allocator at boot (wave 15:
      these were MAX_HARTS = 8 static slots, 64 KiB of .bss at the default
      whatever the board had).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WCET measurement table
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config WCET_MAX_POINTS
    int "Maximum WCET measurement points (fixed + per-function)"
    range 16 1024
    default 32   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Total slots in the WCET measurement table.  The first 9 are
      reserved for the existing fixed points (pid_loop, sensor_read,
      ctx_switch, timer_isr, actuator_write, net_send, cnn_infer,
      lidar_scan, path_plan); the rest are filled by `#[wcet(...)]`
      attribute annotations enumerated by crates/drivers/sys/build.rs.
      See RFC-0027.  Sized as a Kconfig option so each deployment
      profile can tune the budget without a source-code change.

      Cost: 36 B of RAM per point (WCET, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64.  At the default: edge (128) 4,608 B; embedded (32) 1,152 B;
      fleet (512) 18,432 B.  At least 9 (the fixed points); the Kconfig range
      starts at 16.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ring-3 driver supervisor (RFC-0049 M4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SUP_RESTART_BURST
    int "Supervised driver: restarts allowed within the interval"
    range 1 16
    default 3
    help
      The cold-restart supervisor (kernel/src/drv_supervisor.rs) restarts a
      supervised ring-3 driver that died of a failure (killed by a fault,
      a seccomp kill or a kernel stop request, or exited with a code other
      than 0) at most this many times within SUP_RESTART_INTERVAL_S.  The
      failure after that stays down until the machine restarts.  A driver
      that exits 0 is never restarted.  systemd's StartLimitBurst, with
      Restart=on-failure.

config SUP_RESTART_INTERVAL_S
    int "Supervised driver: restart-limit interval (seconds)"
    range 1 86400
    default 300
    help
      The window SUP_RESTART_BURST counts restarts in: a restart older than
      this no longer counts against the budget, so a driver that fails
      rarely is restarted every time and one in a crash loop is given up
      on.  systemd's StartLimitIntervalSec.  The default leaves the gate's
      four-kill supervisor row (worst case ~80 s under host load) inside
      one window.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Process exit, io_ring, filesystems (wave 11: were literals in the crates)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_IO_RINGS
    int "Maximum io_rings system-wide"
    range 2 256
    default 16
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/io_ring.rs, MAX_IO_RINGS. One task may hold
      half of them. At most 256: a Cap<IoRing> packs the index in 8 bits
      (a compile-time assertion in io_ring.rs). Each ring is one 4 KiB
      shared page, allocated when the ring is created.

      Cost: 268 B of static RAM per ring slot (the ring table, 68 B, and its
      parked-waiter set, 200 B, .data and .bss: 4,296 B at the default 16),
      the same on riscv64 and aarch64; plus one 4 KiB page-allocator frame
      for each ring that exists, so up to 64 KiB of frames at 16 rings, 1 MiB
      at 256.
      Ceiling: 256 (a compile-time assertion in io_ring.rs: the Cap<IoRing>
      index is 8 bits); the Kconfig range already stops there.

config VFS_MAX_FILES
    int "Maximum inodes in the in-kernel RAM filesystem"
    range 64 4096
    default 128
    help
      Size of the VFS inode pool (crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs, MAX_FILES):
      every file and directory of the RAM filesystem, plus the inodes a
      mounted filesystem's open files take.

      Cost: 160 B of RAM per inode (FS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 20,480 B of the 20,888-byte filesystem table at the default 128
      (the mount table and a few counters are the rest).  No code ceiling below the
      range.

config VFS_MAX_MOUNTS
    int "Maximum mounted filesystems"
    range 4 16
    default 4
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs, MAX_MOUNTS.

      Cost: 96 B of RAM per mount slot (FS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 384 B at the default 4.

config FS_BLOCK_CACHE_KB
    int "FAT32 block cache (KiB; 0 = the 4 KiB, 8-sector cache)"
    range 0 16384
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 512  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, SECTOR_CACHE (crates/fs/fs/src/bcache.rs):
      the FAT32 driver's cache of 512-byte volume sectors, directory and FAT
      sectors and file data alike, set-associative (8 ways) past 8 lines.
      A repeated open/read of a small file, or a repeated spawn of the same
      image, is served from it instead of the block device.  Write-through:
      every write reaches the device before it returns, in the same order as
      without the cache; writes by anyone else (USB mass-storage gadget,
      reserved-tail records, SYS_DISK_WRITE) drop the sectors they touch
      (azos_drv_block::write_observer).  0 or a power of two from 4; 0
      keeps the 8 x 512 B cache this option replaced.

      Cost: the value in KiB of RAM (.bss, not image bytes) plus 32 B of tags
      per 512 B line (6.25 %), the same on riscv64 and aarch64: 544 KiB at
      edge's 512, 68 KiB at embedded's 64.

config FAT32_SECTOR_CLAIM_SLOTS
    int "FAT32 FAT-sector claim slots (1-64; concurrent FAT updates)"
    range 1 64
    default 64 if PROFILE_FLEET || PROFILE_EDGE
    default 64 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, FAT_SECTOR_BUSY: a FAT entry update (cluster
      allocation, free, chain link) reads a FAT sector, patches 4 bytes and
      writes it to every FAT copy. No mutex is held across that device I/O
      (an RT waiter must not inherit disk latency through priority
      inheritance); instead the update claims its FAT sector in a one-word
      bitmap, slot = sector % this value, and a second updater of the same
      slot sleeps on a wait queue (no PI) until the first publishes.
      Updates to different slots run concurrently.  1 serialises every FAT
      update (the pre-F1 behaviour, without PI); 64 gives the most
      concurrency.

      Cost: none in RAM (the bitmap is one u64 whatever the value) and a
      couple of atomic instructions per FAT entry update, the same on
      riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64. Lower it only to measure contention.

config PI_TRACE_DEPTH
    int "PiMutex trace: nesting levels recorded per task (diagnostics)"
    range 1 16
    default 4 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 4
    help
      crates/core/sync/src/pi_mutex.rs, PI_HELD_ADDR: with the
      `pi-held-trace` cargo feature (pulled in by the `lat-fat` smoke), the
      address of each PiMutex a task holds is kept for the first this-many
      nesting levels, so an F1 report (a device wait under a PiMutex) names
      the lock. Deeper levels still count; their address is not kept.

      Cost: only in a diagnostic build: 64 x this x 8 B of .bss (2 KiB at 4)
      and one store per PiMutex acquisition, on every ISA. Raise it only if a
      report shows a nesting deeper than 4.

config FAT_PI_IO_SITES
    int "FAT32 F1 probe: distinct (task, lock) sites recorded (diagnostics)"
    range 1 64
    default 8 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 8
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, PI_IO_SITE: with the `fat-pi-io-probe`
      cargo feature (the `lat-fat` smoke), each FAT32 device I/O issued under
      a held PiMutex is attributed to (task, outermost lock, innermost lock);
      this many distinct sites are kept and counted, the rest only add to
      `pi_io_all`.

      Cost: only in a diagnostic build: 24 B of .bss per site and a scan of
      this many entries per offending I/O (none when F1 holds), on every ISA.

config LOG_RING_ENTRIES
    int "Flight-recorder ring entries (power of two)"
    range 16 4096
    default 64 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 128
    help
      crates/core/actuation/src/logger.rs: records the flight recorder holds
      in RAM until the log flusher writes them. The ring is lock-free (one
      compare-and-swap per record, many producers, one consumer): a
      real-time task logs into it and never waits. Full, a new record
      evicts the oldest, counted in the logger's `events_dropped`. Must be
      a power of two.

      Cost: 40 bytes of RAM per entry (32-byte record + sequence word),
      5 KiB at 128; no instructions depend on it. Raise it when
      `events_dropped` climbs between two flushes; embedded boards
      default to 64.

config LOG_FLUSHER_PRIORITY
    int "Log flusher task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 12 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 12
    help
      kernel/src/boot/sched.rs, `log-flush`: the kernel task that writes
      the flight recorder's ring to disk and runs the block I/O that
      real-time tasks hand it (owner rule: an RT task never does block
      I/O). Woken by every flush request; an RT task's `*_durable` record
      is on disk once this task has run one flush after the request, so
      its priority is that bound. 12 is the most urgent priority outside
      the RT band (time-sliced, so it cannot starve the band's peers).

      Cost: one kernel task (its stack, KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB) on every
      ISA. Lower it (a larger number) only if logging must yield to
      every other normal task.

config LOG_FLUSHER_JOBS
    int "Deferred block-I/O job slots on the log flusher"
    range 1 16
    default 4 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 4
    help
      One-shot jobs a real-time task hands the log flusher instead of
      doing block I/O itself (the OTA boot-good mark from the RT system
      watchdog). A full table refuses the job and the RT task retries on
      its next pass.

      Cost: one machine word of RAM per slot; the flusher scans them on
      every pass.

config VIRTIO_BLK_INFLIGHT
    int "virtio-blk requests in flight at once (1-5)"
    range 1 5
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 4
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/blk.rs: each in-flight request owns
      a staging slot (request header, 4 KiB bounce buffer, status byte).
      A request is submitted under the driver's lock and waited for
      without it (owner rule F1: no lock held across device I/O), so up to
      this many tasks have requests in the device at once; one more sleeps
      for a free slot. Five is the most the 16-entry virtqueue holds (three
      descriptors per request).

      Cost: 4 KiB + 17 bytes of RAM per slot; nothing per request beyond
      the reaper's scan of this many slots. 1 serialises every request
      (the pre-F1 behaviour, without the lock across the wait).

config EXEC_IMAGE_CACHE_KB
    int "Verified exec image cache (KiB of frames; 0 = off)"
    range 0 65536
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 512  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      crates/core/syscall/src/image_cache.rs, crates/core/mm/src/image_frames.rs:
      the frames a spawn of a FAT32 image keeps for the next spawn of the
      same bytes (same start cluster and size, and no write of the volume,
      mount or unmount since; USB mass storage, OTA and SYS_DISK_WRITE
      writes count). The next spawn maps the executable pages read-execute,
      shared, and copies every other page from a kernel template, so it
      neither reads nor hashes the file. Writable pages are never shared;
      every page is present when the task starts (a mem = "locked" row
      stays committed). Least recently used images go first; a frame a
      running task still maps outlives its eviction until that task exits.
      0 keeps only the digest cache (16 entries, always on).

      Cost: nothing in the image (.bss: about 1 KiB of table); at run time
      up to this many KiB of frames from the page allocator, held only by
      images spawned at least once: one frame per image page plus one per
      image. A task that maps a kept image is charged every page of it, as
      if it had loaded it.

config TMPFS_MAX_FILES
    int "Maximum files in tmpfs"
    range 8 1024
    default 64
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/tmpfs.rs, TMPFS_MAX_FILES.

      Cost: 96 B of RAM per entry (TMPFS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 6,168 B at the default 64.  File data is not in this table (see
      TMPFS_MAX_KB).

config TMPFS_MAX_KB
    int "tmpfs data capacity (KiB)"
    range 64 65536
    default 2048
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/tmpfs.rs, TMPFS_MAX_BYTES. A write that would pass
      it evicts the oldest file first (FIFO).

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs).  File data is allocated
      from the kernel heap when a file is written, so the worst case is
      TMPFS_MAX_KB of KERNEL_HEAP_SIZE (2 MiB of edge's 32 MiB heap) plus a
      transient second copy of the file being rewritten.  Embedded's heap is
      512 KiB, so there the heap fills before this ceiling does and a write
      fails with an allocation error.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# User shell (RFC-0055, wave 11)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config USER_SHELL
    bool
    default y if !CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE
    help
      Derived from CONSOLE_PROGRAM (Userspace programs): y when a ring-3
      program is started on the console at boot (by default the native
      shell, userspace/services/sh, /fat/SH.ELF, supervised like the ring-3
      drivers: its topology row says start = true). The in-kernel shell
      becomes a recovery console: it runs only in safe mode, when the volume
      has no console image, when the console program does not take the
      console within SH_START_TIMEOUT_S, or after the supervisor gave up on
      it.

      n (CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE): the kernel shell owns the console, as before
      wave 11.

config SH_START_TIMEOUT_S
    int "User shell: seconds to take the console before the recovery console does"
    range 1 300
    default 10
    help
      After boot, and after each death of the user shell, the kernel
      console waits this long for SH.ELF to claim console input. If it
      has not, the recovery console (the in-kernel shell) takes it.

config KERNEL_CMDLINE_INIT
    bool "Honour init=/fat/NAME.ELF on the kernel command line (secure boot off)"
    default y
    help
      The kernel command line (the device tree's /chosen/bootargs; QEMU
      -append) may name the console program for one boot:
      `init=/fat/NAME.ELF`, an 8.3 image directly under /fat whose topology
      row exists (spawn refuses an image without one; the row's seccomp
      profile, capabilities and supervision apply). It replaces
      CONSOLE_PROGRAM for that boot. Never with secure boot on
      (SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED): the word is ignored and the boot log says so,
      since the console program is part of what the signed configuration
      decides. Costs one device-tree walk at boot.

      n: init= is ignored on every build (the log says so).

config KERNEL_CMDLINE_MAX
    int "Kernel command line: bytes read from /chosen/bootargs"
    range 64 4096
    default 256
    help
      The command line is copied onto the boot stack once, at this length;
      longer is cut (and an init= past the cut is not seen). Costs this
      many bytes of the boot CPU's stack while it is read.

endmenu # Resource Limits
